description:
event.rs:949-957 (blobIds) and :959-966 (properties) parse arrays with filter_map, silently dropping non-string entries:

  let blob_ids: Vec<Id> = args_map
      .get("blobIds")
      .and_then(|v| v.as_array())
      .map(|arr| {
          arr.iter()
              .filter_map(|v| v.as_str().map(Id::from))   // <-- silent drop
              .collect()
      })
      .unwrap_or_default();

This is inconsistent with the same crate's destroy-array validation, which explicitly rejects non-string entries (calendar.rs:238-242, event.rs:436-440, event_notification.rs:116-120, participant_identity.rs:213-217):

  if let Some(bad) = destroy_arr.iter().find(|v| !v.is_string()) {
      return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(
          "destroy: every element must be a string Id; got {bad}"
      )));
  }

A client sending blobIds: ["blob1", null, "blob2"] gets a successful response with blob2 silently dropped — no notFound, no notParsable, no method error. The same client sending destroy: ["id1", null, "id2"] gets an explicit invalidArguments error. The behaviour should be consistent across both array-of-string arguments.

RFC 8620 §3.6.1 mandates invalidArguments for malformed args; filter_map-with-silent-drop violates that. The pattern is also unidiomatic in Rust: filter_map is for cases where 'this entry shouldn't contribute' is the expected semantic. For a validation-style 'every entry must satisfy a type contract', the idiomatic shape is either:

(a) Collect into Result<Vec<Id>, JmapError>:
       arr.iter()
          .map(|v| v.as_str().map(Id::from).ok_or_else(|| JmapError::invalid_arguments("blobIds: entry must be a string")))
          .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()?

(b) Mirror the destroy-array pre-validation pattern.

Either matches the rest of this crate. The current shape teaches the wrong invariant.
